### Meeting Minutes

**Team Roles and Semester Planning**
*   **Roles:** Jeongjoon (PM/Mini-games), Xiyin & Anlin (Art/UI/UX), Peter (Game Design), Wing (Data pipeline), and Shaoming (Godot development).
*   **Sprints:** Sprint 4 (Week 1–3) focuses on refining and prototyping; Sprint 2 on core mechanisms; Sprint 3 on internal/external testing; and Sprint 4 on final polishing and presentation.

**Track A — Cludo Game Concept (Jeongjoon)**
*   **Content:** A Cludo-style deduction game to find five treasures (e.g., rubber duck, Linux window) within building interiors.
*   **Mechanism:** Players make guesses based on cards to find the correct combination of room and treasure within a limited number of turns.

**Track B — Outdoor Game Concept (Peter)**v
*   **Content:** A DND-style simulation of ANU life using a character sheet.
*   **Mechanism:** Players manage attributes (Energy, Budget, Luck) and choose majors/residences (e.g., Bruce Hall, UniLodge). Found treasures are documented in an Instagram-style diary.

**Technical Progress (Shaoming & Art Team)**
*   **Development:** Basic character movement on a Chifley Library tile map has been implemented in the Godot engine.
*   **Assets:** Environmental assets (bushes, trees, roads) and building models for the Chifley and CS building districts are prepared.

**Client Feedback & Integration Strategy**
*   **Simplification:** The client recommended removing complex character sheets and dice-rolling mechanics to reduce coding burden and improve accessibility.
*   **Decision-Based Design:** Shift toward a "Telltale Games" style focusing on meaningful "yes/no" decisions and storytelling consequences.
*   **Time to Fun (TTF):** Ensure the game is approachable for non-gamers by minimizing the time it takes to reach interesting gameplay.
*   **Blending Tracks:** The outdoor map could serve as a node-based "fast travel" system leading into specific indoor encounters.

